<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-factors-multiples-primes">
  <title>Factors and Multiples</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-factors-multiples"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Recognise factors, multiples, primes and composite numbers.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the prime factorisation of a positive integer.</p></li>
      <li><p>Calculate the highest common factor and lowest common multiple.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Factors and multiples reveal the multiplicative structure of integers. Prime factorisation allows systematic calculations.</p></introduction>

  
<section xml:id="number-multiples-foundations"><title>Recognising Multiples</title>
<p>Multiplication is a way of counting equal groups. Four groups of three contain twelve objects. We write <m>4\times3=12</m>. Twelve is a multiple of both three and four.</p>
<definition xml:id="definition-positive-multiple"><statement><p>A positive multiple of a positive whole number is obtained by multiplying it by a positive whole number. Zero is also a multiple, since multiplying by zero gives zero.</p></statement></definition>
<example><title>Multiples of Four</title><p>Count in fours: <m>4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,40</m>. These are the first ten positive multiples of four.</p></example>
<exercise><statement><p>Write the first six positive multiples of three. Which are also multiples of two?</p></statement><answer><p>3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18. The multiples of two are 6, 12 and 18.</p></answer></exercise>
<warning><title>Make This Mistake for the Last Time</title><p>Do not confuse a factor with a multiple. Since <m>4\times6=24</m>, four is a factor of twenty-four, and twenty-four is a multiple of four.</p></warning>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-divisibility-foundations"><title>Recognising Divisibility</title>
<definition xml:id="definition-divisibility-foundations"><statement><p>A whole number is divisible by a positive whole number if the division leaves no remainder.</p></statement></definition>
<example><title>Exact Division</title><p><m>24\div4=6</m>, so 24 is divisible by 4. But <m>25=4\times6+1</m>, so 25 is not divisible by 4.</p></example>
<p>We can often recognise divisibility without carrying out the division.</p>
<table><title>Useful divisibility rules</title><tabular>
<row><cell>Divisor</cell><cell>Test</cell><cell>Example</cell></row>
<row><cell>2</cell><cell>Last digit is even.</cell><cell>138</cell></row>
<row><cell>3</cell><cell>Sum of digits is divisible by 3.</cell><cell>123: 1+2+3=6.</cell></row>
<row><cell>4</cell><cell>Number formed by the last two digits is divisible by 4.</cell><cell>316: 16 is divisible by 4.</cell></row>
<row><cell>5</cell><cell>Last digit is 0 or 5.</cell><cell>145</cell></row>
<row><cell>9</cell><cell>Sum of digits is divisible by 9.</cell><cell>729: 7+2+9=18.</cell></row>
<row><cell>10</cell><cell>Last digit is 0.</cell><cell>270</cell></row>
</tabular></table>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-divisibility-by-two-five-ten"><title>Divisibility by 2, 5 and 10</title>
<p>Look at the units digit: 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8 for divisibility by two; 0 or 5 for divisibility by five; 0 for divisibility by ten.</p>
<example><title>Large Numbers</title><p>34 670 is divisible by all three. 84 315 is divisible by five but not by two or ten. 12 348 is divisible by two but not by five or ten.</p></example>
<exercise xml:id="quiz-divisibility-five-choice"><title>Easy — Which Number Is Not Divisible by 5?</title><statement><p>Choose the number that is not divisible by five.</p></statement><choices><choice><statement><p>23 450</p></statement><feedback><p>The last digit is zero.</p></feedback></choice><choice correct="yes"><statement><p>84 312</p></statement><feedback><p>The units digit is two, not zero or five.</p></feedback></choice><choice><statement><p>67 895</p></statement><feedback><p>The last digit is five.</p></feedback></choice><choice><statement><p>102 005</p></statement><feedback><p>The last digit is five.</p></feedback></choice></choices></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-divisibility-by-three"><title>Divisibility by 3</title>
<theorem xml:id="theorem-divisibility-three"><title>Divisibility by 3</title><statement><p><idx>Divisibility by 3</idx>A whole number is divisible by three if and only if the sum of its digits is divisible by three.</p></statement></theorem>
<insight xml:id="heuristic-divisibility-three"><title>Heuristic — Crossing Out Digits</title><p><idx>Crossing-out method</idx>Simplify the digit sum by crossing out digits or groups of digits whose sum is a multiple of three. First remove 0, 3, 6 and 9. Next look for pairs such as 1 and 8, 2 and 7, or 4 and 5. Add the remaining digits. If nothing remains, the sum is zero and the number is divisible by three.</p></insight>
<example xml:id="example-divisibility-three-pairs"><title>Testing 7245 for Divisibility by 3</title><p>Both pairs sum to nine. Cross them out.</p><me>\underbrace{\cancel{7}\quad\cancel{2}}_{9}\qquad\underbrace{\cancel{4}\quad\cancel{5}}_{9}</me><p>Nothing remains. Therefore 7245 is divisible by three. Check using your calculator: <m>7245\div3=2415</m>.</p></example>
<example xml:id="example-divisibility-three-large"><title>Testing a Larger Number</title><p>Test 73920645. Cross out 3, 9, 0 and 6 first. Then pair 7 with 2, and 4 with 5.</p><me>\cancel{7}\ \cancel{3}\ \cancel{9}\ \cancel{2}\ \cancel{0}\ \cancel{6}\ \cancel{4}\ \cancel{5}</me><p>Nothing remains, so the number is divisible by three.</p></example>
<example xml:id="example-divisibility-three-remainder"><title>When Digits Remain</title><p>Test 72458. Cross out the pairs 7 and 2, and 4 and 5.</p><me>\underbrace{\cancel{7}\quad\cancel{2}}_{9}\qquad\underbrace{\cancel{4}\quad\cancel{5}}_{9}\qquad 8</me><p>Eight remains. It is not divisible by three, so neither is 72458.</p></example>
<warning xml:id="warning-divisibility-three"><title>Make This Mistake for the Last Time</title><p>Checking only the final digit is insufficient. For example, 13 ends in 3 but is not divisible by 3. The crossing-out method simplifies a digit sum; it does not mean that deleting digits preserves the original number.</p></warning>
<exercise xml:id="practice-divisibility-three-easy"><title>Easy — Recognise Multiples</title><statement><p>Without a calculator, identify which of 45, 52, 81, 103 and 126 are divisible by three. Then verify by division on your calculator.</p></statement><answer><p>45, 81 and 126.</p></answer></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="practice-divisibility-three-medium"><title>Intermediate — Cross Out Digits</title><statement><p>Use the crossing-out method on 72945, 804217, 632718 and 9245708. Record the remaining digit sum.</p></statement><solution><p>The remaining sums can be 0, 4, 0 and 8 respectively. The first and third numbers are divisible by three. For 804217, remove 0, pair 8 with 1 and 2 with 7, leaving 4. For 9245708, remove 9 and 0, pair 2 with 7 and 4 with 5, leaving 8.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="practice-divisibility-three-hard"><title>Challenging — Find the Missing Digit</title><statement><p>Find every possible digit that can replace the box in <m>72\Box45</m> so that the number is divisible by three. Explain how you know your list is complete.</p></statement><solution><p>The known digits form two pairs summing to nine. The missing digit must itself be divisible by three, so the possibilities are 0, 3, 6 and 9.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="quiz-divisibility-three-choice"><title>Check Your Understanding</title><statement><p>Which number is not divisible by three?</p></statement><choices><choice><statement><p>7245</p></statement><feedback><p>Both pairs sum to nine.</p></feedback></choice><choice correct="yes"><statement><p>72458</p></statement><feedback><p>The pairs sum to nine, but eight remains.</p></feedback></choice><choice><statement><p>9306</p></statement><feedback><p>Every digit is divisible by three.</p></feedback></choice><choice><statement><p>8127</p></statement><feedback><p>Pair 8 with 1 and 2 with 7.</p></feedback></choice></choices></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-divisibility-by-four"><title>Divisibility by 4</title>
<example><title>Is 732 Divisible by Three and Four?</title><p>The sum of its digits is <m>7+3+2=12</m>, a multiple of three. The last two digits form 32, a multiple of four. Therefore 732 is divisible by both three and four.</p></example>
<warning><title>Make This Mistake for the Last Time</title><p>A number ending in four need not be divisible by four. For example, 14 is not divisible by four. Check the last two digits, not just the last digit.</p></warning>
<exercise><title>Which Statement Is Wrong?</title><statement><p>Exactly one statement below is false. Identify it, explain the error and correct it.</p><ol><li><p>126 is divisible by 3.</p></li><li><p>234 is divisible by 4.</p></li><li><p>175 is divisible by 5.</p></li></ol></statement><solution><p>The second statement is false: 34 is not divisible by four. For instance, 236 is divisible by four because 36 is.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise><title>Independent Practice</title><statement><p>For each number, identify which of 2, 3, 4, 5, 9 and 10 divide it exactly: 48, 75, 108, 130, 243, 360.</p></statement><answer><p>48: 2, 3, 4. 75: 3, 5. 108: 2, 3, 4, 9. 130: 2, 5, 10. 243: 3, 9. 360: all six.</p></answer></exercise>

<aside xml:id="explore-further-divisibility-four"><title>Explore Further (Optional): Why Check the Last Two Digits?</title><p>Every whole number can be written as <m>100q+r</m>, where <m>r</m> is the number formed by its last two digits. Since 100 is divisible by four, only the remainder <m>r</m> matters. This explains the divisibility rule for four.</p></aside>
</section>

  <section xml:id="number-divisibility-by-nine"><title>Divisibility by 9</title><p>A number is divisible by nine if its digit sum is divisible by nine. Cross out zero, nine and groups summing to nine or another multiple of nine. Unlike the test for three, do not discard a single three or six.</p><example><p>For 7245, both pairs sum to nine, so the number is divisible by nine. For 72453, three remains, so it is divisible by three but not by nine.</p></example><exercise><statement><p>Which of 918, 936, 732 and 72 453 are divisible by nine?</p></statement><answer><p>918 and 936.</p></answer></exercise></section>
<section xml:id="number-divisibility-optional"><title>Further Divisibility Rules (Optional)</title><aside xml:id="explore-further-divisibility-seven"><title>Explore Further (Optional): Divisibility by Seven</title><p>Double the last digit and subtract it from the remaining number. Repeat if necessary. The original number is divisible by seven exactly when the result is divisible by seven.</p><p>For 91, calculate <m>9-2(1)=7</m>. For 1729, calculate <m>172-18=154</m>, then <m>15-8=7</m>. Both numbers are divisible by seven.</p><p>Try 203, 245, 319 and 1001. Predict first, then check using your calculator. The answers are yes, yes, no and yes respectively.</p><p><em>Why does it work?</em> Write the original number as <m>N=10a+b</m>. Then <m>N=10(a-2b)+21b</m>. Since 21 is divisible by seven and 10 has no common factor with seven other than 1, <m>N</m> is divisible by seven exactly when <m>a-2b</m> is divisible by seven.</p></aside></section>
<section xml:id="factors-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>A factor divides a whole number without a remainder; a multiple is obtained by multiplying it by an integer. A prime number greater than one has exactly two positive factors, 1 and itself.</p></introduction>
    <p>Prime factorisation reveals the structure of positive integers. The <term>highest common factor</term> (HCF) uses the prime factors shared by the numbers, each with the lower power; the <term>lowest common multiple</term> (LCM) uses every prime factor needed, each with the higher power.</p>
    <p>For example, <m>12=2^2\cdot3</m> and <m>18=2\cdot3^2</m>. Their HCF is <m>2\cdot3=6</m>; their LCM is <m>2^2\cdot3^2=36</m>. The two ideas answer different questions: what divides both, and what is divisible by both?</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="factors-multiples-primes-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-factors-multiples-primes">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Express 60 as a product of prime factors.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>60=2×2×3×5, or 2²×3×5.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-factors-multiples-primes">
      <statement><p>Find the HCF of 12 and 18.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The common factors are 1, 2, 3 and 6, so the HCF is 6.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  <aside xml:id="explore-further-divisor-functions"><title>Explore Further — Counting Prime Factors and Divisors (Optional)</title><p>This enrichment is not required for the examination or compulsory assessment.</p><p>For <m>n=p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_r^{a_r}</m>, the function <m>\Omega(n)=a_1+\cdots+a_r</m> counts prime factors with multiplicity, while <m>\omega(n)=r</m> counts distinct primes. The divisor function is <m>\tau(n)=(a_1+1)\cdots(a_r+1)</m>, because each exponent in a divisor can be chosen independently from zero to its maximum value.</p><p>For example, <m>72=2^3\cdot3^2</m> gives <m>\Omega(72)=5</m>, <m>\omega(72)=2</m> and <m>\tau(72)=12</m>. A sphenic number is the product of three distinct primes, such as <m>30=2\cdot3\cdot5</m>. It has eight positive divisors.</p></aside>
</section>
<outcomes><title>Review Your Learning</title><introduction><p>Can you now do each of the following independently?</p></introduction><ol><li><p>Recognise multiples, factors and prime numbers.</p></li><li><p>Test divisibility by 2, 3, 4, 5, 9 and 10.</p></li><li><p>Express a positive integer as a product of prime factors.</p></li><li><p>Calculate the HCF and LCM.</p></li></ol></outcomes>
</chapter>
